    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d669">

                <pb facs="0611" n="605" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg605"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.20/474½</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by President Roosevelt to the Under
                        Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Welles</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-07-26">July 26, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I do not agree with this estimate of the situation.<anchor n="40"
                        xml:id="d669-f1"/> There is, of course, always danger of some internal
                    trouble in Liberia but the fact of possessing even two or three line mobile
                    field pieces is going to keep a raiding enemy cruiser or submarine out of the
                    roadstead. The cost can be made negligible by cutting red tape.</p>
                <closer><signed>F[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ranklin</hi>] D. R[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">oosevelt</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
